life is slowly returning to normal in  under outside of durban three days after  torrential rain and flooding caused  widespread damage this is the first day  clonipani pakamani has been able to  operate his food business which he  relies on for a daily income  the floods have really affected us the  people who used to support us could not  come and buy because they were not even  going to work  even the little money they had they had  to use elsewhere  with the water and electricity supplies  still cut off clonipani does not have  anywhere to keep his stock city  officials are yet to begin clearing and  rebuilding some of the roads and bridges  which collapsed were destroyed by  mudslides  at a home tola kelem swami's cleaning up  she says as the flood level reached as  high as the rooftop her five children  just managed to make it out  safely lost everything my kids birth  certificates school books and clothes my  fridge my television nothing's working  as you can see around here and i've not  received any help i only managed to get  blankets from my neighbor  resident notando  says some food has been distributed but  people in this ward have not received  any and they have to make use of  whatever they can to get by  most of my community members are using  the stream where the water we don't even  know where it's coming from so to wash  our laundry and also drink from this  contaminated water  while people try to recover it could be  short-lived more rain is expected in the  coming days  many people living in informal  settlements like this one already  struggle to make ends meet the  destruction caused by the flooding has  set them back even further and many say  they've not received help from the  government that's despite its promises  to assist those affected familia miller  al jazeera durban south africa
 

